---
import DocLayout from "../layouts/DocLayout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Table from "../components/Table.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
---

<DocLayout title="Dynamic DOM integration" description="SPA routes, late targets, and missing elements.">
  <h1>Dynamic DOM integration</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The hard case for a tour library: the router tore down the element you were pointing at, and
    new markup arrives a frame later.
  </p>

  <h2>The default already handles it</h2>

  <p>
    <code>target.missing</code> defaults to <code>"wait"</code>, which waits for the target using a
    MutationObserver plus a slow polling interval — the observer catches insertions, the interval
    catches visibility changes that do not mutate the DOM.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  target: { missing: "wait", timeout: 5000 },
  steps: [
    // Mounted only after the user opens the panel.
    { tourId: "app.panel", title: "Settings", content: "Everything is here." },
  ],
});`}
  />

  <p>
    The wait is always cancellable. If the tour is destroyed while waiting, the observer is
    disconnected, the timer cleared, and the pending promise rejected — nothing is left running.
  </p>

  <h2>Across route changes</h2>

  <p>
    A tour that spans routes works, because every step re-resolves its target when it is presented.
    Combine <code>missing: "wait"</code> with a generous timeout.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`const tour = createTour({
  tourId: "spa-tour",
  waitForTarget: true,
  target: { missing: "wait" },
  steps: [
    { tourId: "route.overview", title: "Start here", content: "On the first page." },
    // Tell the user what to do while the library waits for the next page.
    { tourId: "route.builder", title: "Now open Reports", content: "This step waits for the new route." },
    { tourId: "run.schedule", title: "Schedule a run", content: "Also on the Reports page." },
  ],
});

// Give up gracefully if the user never navigates.
setTimeout(() => {
  if (tour.getState() === "running") tour.stop("timeout");
}, 60_000);`}
  />

  <Callout title="Waiting is not scrolling">
    <p>
      Waiting for a target says nothing about scrolling. If the element is far below the fold, also
      set <code>scrollIntoView: true</code> so it is brought into view once it appears.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>Handling the navigation yourself</h2>

  <p>
    If a tour only makes sense on some routes, drive it from the router. This is usually better UX
    than making every step wait.
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`// React Router
useEffect(() => {
  if (!ONBOARDING_ROUTES.includes(location.pathname)) {
    tourRef.current?.stop("left-route");
    return;
  }
  if (location.pathname === "/dashboard") {
    tourRef.current?.start();
  }
}, [location.pathname]);

// Vue Router
watch(() => route.path, (path) => {
  if (path === "/dashboard") tour.start();
  else if (tour.isActive()) tour.stop("left-route");
});`}
  />

  <h2>Per-step strategies</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`steps: [
  // Optional UI: show it centred if the panel is not open.
  { tourId: "app.panel", title: "Settings", content: "…", target: { missing: "fallback" } },

  // Not essential: skip it entirely.
  { tourId: "app.beta", title: "Beta", content: "…", target: { missing: "skip" } },

  // Essential but occasionally slow: wait, then fail loudly.
  { tourId: "app.checkout", title: "Checkout", content: "…",
    target: { missing: "wait" }, timeout: 10000 },

  // Should always be there: a bug if it is not.
  { tourId: "app.header", title: "Header", content: "…", target: { missing: "error" } },
]`}
  />

  <Table
    head={["Strategy", "Behaviour", "Use when"]}
    rows={[
      [
        "<code>wait</code> <span class=\"pill\">default</span>",
        "Wait up to the timeout, then throw.",
        "The element is coming, reliably, in a known timeframe.",
      ],
      [
        "<code>skip</code>",
        "Advance past the step.",
        "The step is optional and its absence is acceptable.",
      ],
      [
        "<code>fallback</code>",
        "Show the step centred, no spotlight.",
        "The message still matters even without the element.",
      ],
      [
        "<code>error</code>",
        "Throw a <code>TargetError</code>.",
        "A missing element is a bug you want to hear about.",
      ],
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Per-step timeouts</h2>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`createTour({
  tourId: "x",
  target: { missing: "wait", timeout: 3000 },  // default
  steps: [
    { tourId: "fast", title: "…", timeout: 1000 },
    { tourId: "slow", title: "…", timeout: 15000 },
  ],
});`}
  />

  <h2>Waiting on something the router controls</h2>

  <p>
    When you know the mount is imminent, tell the engine directly through <code>beforeStep</code>
    rather than making it poll:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`tour.on("beforeStep", async ({ step }) => {
  if (step.tourId === "route.builder") {
    await router.navigate("/reports");
  }
});`}
  />

  <h2>Shadow DOM</h2>

  <p>
    <code>querySelectorAll</code> does not cross a shadow boundary, and neither can this library. If
    your app uses web components, pass the element directly:
  </p>

  <CodeBlock
    lang="js"
    code={`const host = document.querySelector("my-component");
const shadow = host.shadowRoot;

createTour({
  tourId: "shadow-tour",
  steps: [{ title: "Inside the component", target: { element: shadow.querySelector(".cta") } }],
});`}
  />

  <Callout title="Cross-origin iframes" variant="warn">
    <p>
      Not supported, and not planned. A tour cannot resolve, measure or position an element in a
      different document.
    </p>
  </Callout>
</DocLayout>